anch `local-agent/j-20261016-33f257`). I read its diff against the base commit, and it matched the spec. I brought it in with `git merge --ff-only`.\n\n- **`sqlite_utils/db.py`:** `transform` and `transform_sql` take `strict=None`. `strict=True` raises `TransformError` when SQLite lacks STRICT support. After a successful `transform`, the mode is stored in `self._defaults[\"strict\"]`.\n- **`sqlite_utils/cli.py`:** `transform` has a `--strict/--no-strict` option that is passed to both the `--sql` path and the normal path.\n- **Docs:** `docs/python-api.rst` has the new \"Changing strict mode\" section, and `docs/cli.rst` lists `--strict` and `--no-strict`.\n\nI did not change the transaction handling. The spec asked to check that a failed conversion to strict leaves the original table intact with no temporary table left over. The worker reported that the existing `atomic()` handling covers this and that the tests exercise it. I didn't check that separately.\n\nThe merge was a fast-forward, so the changes are in the working tree but not committed by me.", "ttft_ms": 2530, "type": "result", "duration_ms": 80516, "uuid": "0579e42c-6c8a-46b0-be3e-8a737820826e", "ttft_stream_ms": 1572, "time_to_request_ms": 55, "first_content_frame_ms": 1573, "queued_turn_count": 0, "result_index": 0}
